1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,720
How can ecom take a card payment without ever seeing the card?

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:07,488
In a real shop, the card reader on the counter belongs to the bank.

3
00:00:07,538 --> 00:00:10,680
You type your PIN into it, and the shop only gets a receipt.

4
00:00:10,730 --> 00:00:13,008
Our payment page works the same way.

5
00:00:13,058 --> 00:00:20,856
When a buyer opens the page, ecom asks the card zone for a card-entry session: a short-lived place to type one card for one payment.

6
00:00:20,906 --> 00:00:23,136
The card zone answers with an address.

7
00:00:23,186 --> 00:00:28,800
The page shows that address inside a frame: the bank's card reader, on ecom's counter.

8
00:00:28,850 --> 00:00:33,216
The buyer types the card into the frame, and the card goes straight to the card zone.

9
00:00:33,266 --> 00:00:36,408
The page gets back only the token and the masked number.

10
00:00:36,458 --> 00:00:43,248
Then ecom asks the card zone to charge the card by its token, and checks that the token really came from this payment's session.

11
00:00:43,298 --> 00:00:49,704
The two products agree on every question they may ask each other, written down once: a contract.

12
00:00:49,754 --> 00:00:52,464
ecom can only ask what the contract allows.

13
00:00:52,514 --> 00:00:58,200
And that token can spend money, so ecom still guards it, like a ticket that can fetch a coat.

14
00:00:58,250 --> 00:01:01,224
Next: how every service gets its settings.
